# Wind Utility Data Platform: Sizing & Cost Model

You built sensor-analytics on a laptop. Now scale it to 500 wind
turbines across 3 states. How much data, how much infrastructure,
how much money — and is Databricks the right call?

## The bottom line

| | Annual |
|---|---|
| **Databricks platform (infra only)** | **about $60K** |
| + Platform team (2 data engineers) | about $320K |
| **Total with people** | **about $380K** |
| **DIY alternative** (cloud VM + 5 SaaS tools + glue engineer) | **about $292K** |
| **Risk without a platform** (missed failures + false alarms + regulatory fines) | **about $2.5M** |

The Databricks platform costs about $60K/year in infrastructure
— roughly 2% of the $2.5M risk it mitigates. With people, it's
about $380K — a real cost that needs justification. The DIY
alternative is cheaper (about $292K) but stitches together 5
separate tools with no shared governance or lineage.

**NERC** (North American Electric Reliability Corporation) is the
federal regulator that enforces cybersecurity and reliability
standards for the US power grid. Wind utilities above a certain
size must comply with NERC CIP (Critical Infrastructure
Protection) rules — including proving who has access to sensitive
grid data. Violations carry fines up to $1.54M/day. This is a
major driver of the platform decision.

For a smaller operator (50 turbines, 3 analysts, no NERC
compliance requirements), DuckDB + dbt on a single server is
the right answer.

**The rest of this document shows how we arrived at these numbers.**
Every assumption is explained and sourced. If you disagree with
a number, change it and rerun — that's the point.

---

## Part 1: How Much Data?

### SCADA telemetry

A wind turbine SCADA system monitors 60–100 signals: nacelle
temperature, gearbox vibration, rotor speed, pitch angle, wind
speed/direction, oil pressure, generator temp, power output,
etc. (source: [Pandit et al. 2023](https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1177/0309524X221124031),
reports 60–100 SCADA signals per turbine). We use 100 as a round
number that captures the full sensor suite including derived signals.

turbines = 500
signals_per_turbine = 100

SCADA systems typically poll every 10 minutes (6×/hour × 24 hours
= 144 intervals/day). Some systems poll faster (1–5 min) for
critical signals, but 10 min is the industry standard for routine
condition monitoring (same source — standard SCADA resolution
is 5–10 minutes).

intervals_per_day = 144
readings_per_day = turbines * signals_per_turbine * intervals_per_day

Each reading contains: sensor_id (16 bytes), turbine_id (16 bytes),
timestamp (8 bytes), value (8 bytes as float64), units (8 bytes),
quality_flag (4 bytes), plus overhead. ~100 bytes per row is
typical for columnar IoT schemas.

bytes_per_reading = 100 bytes
raw_daily = readings_per_day * bytes_per_reading

As a daily ingest rate, accumulated over a year:

daily_ingest = raw_daily/day
raw_annual = daily_ingest over 1 year

Parquet stores data in columns, so a column of 7.2M identical
"degrees_c" strings compresses to nearly nothing. Repetitive
sensor data (many sensors reporting similar ranges) compresses
well. 5–10× is typical for columnar formats on IoT data; we
use 8× as a conservative middle.

compression_ratio = 8
parquet_daily = raw_daily / compression_ratio
parquet_annual = raw_annual / compression_ratio

### Other data sources

**Weather:** Regional utilities contract weather data from services
like DTN or Vaisala. 12 stations is typical for a 3-state footprint
(~4 per state). Each station reports ~50 variables (temperature,
humidity, wind at multiple heights, barometric pressure, solar
irradiance, precipitation, etc.) hourly:

weather_daily = 12 * 50 * 24 * 100 bytes / compression_ratio
weather_annual = weather_daily/day over 1 year

**Vibration (CMS):** Condition Monitoring Systems use accelerometers
on bearings, gearboxes, and generators to detect mechanical wear
before it becomes catastrophic (see [DOE gearbox failure research](https://www.energy.gov/eere/wind/articles/zeroing-no-1-cause-wind-turbine-gearbox-failures)). Unlike SCADA's 10-minute
polling, CMS captures high-frequency "bursts" — typically 25 kHz
(25,000 samples/second) for 10 seconds. That produces 250,000 data
points per burst. Each sample is a 4-byte float (acceleration
amplitude). Bursts happen on a schedule (weekly per turbine is
common) or when triggered by anomaly thresholds:

burst_bytes = 250000 * 4 bytes
vibration_weekly = turbines * burst_bytes / compression_ratio
vibration_annual = vibration_weekly/week over 1 year

**Maintenance records:** Work orders from SAP, Maximo, or similar
CMMS (Computerized Maintenance Management System). A 500-turbine
fleet generates roughly 10 work orders per turbine per year
(scheduled inspections, corrective maintenance, blade repairs).
Each work order is ~2 KB of structured data:

maintenance_annual = 5000 * 2 KB

total_annual_data = parquet_annual + weather_annual + vibration_annual + maintenance_annual

### 3-year total with processing copies

In practice you don't store just one copy of your data. A
production pipeline keeps:
- **Raw data** (everything as received, never modified — for
  recovery, auditing, and reprocessing)
- **Cleaned data** (bad readings removed, types validated,
  duplicates dropped — ~80% of raw survives cleaning)
- **Aggregated summaries** (hourly/daily averages that analysts
  actually query — ~30% the size of cleaned data)

That's roughly raw (1×) + cleaned (0.8×) + aggregated (0.3×) =
2.1× the raw volume. You'll learn exactly why in Module 3
(Medallion Architecture):

copy_factor = 2.1
total_3yr_storage = total_annual_data * 3 * copy_factor

**Verdict:** total_3yr_storage as napkin. Fits on a single server.

### How fast can DuckDB scan it?

duckdb_scan = read(total_3yr_storage, ssd)

Full table scan in duckdb_scan. This is NOT a big data problem.

### Ingestion throughput

scada_throughput = daily_ingest
scada_throughput per second
scada_throughput per minute

A 10-gig network link handles throughput(ten_gig). Our SCADA
stream is a rounding error — the network is not the bottleneck.

---

## Part 2: Data Growth

Wind operators grow through acquisitions and new site
construction. US wind capacity has grown ~10% annually over the
past decade (source: ACP/AWEA annual market reports). Applying
that to our fleet's storage:

yr1_storage = total_annual_data * copy_factor
yr3_storage = compound yr1_storage by 10% over 3 years
yr5_storage = compound yr1_storage by 10% over 5 years

---

## Part 3: Databricks Platform Costs

This is what the platform ACTUALLY costs — not just Databricks
DBU charges, but the full cloud bill.

### Cloud storage (S3/ADLS)

AWS S3 Standard: $0.023/GB/month in us-east-1
([AWS S3 pricing](https://aws.amazon.com/s3/pricing/)).
Azure ADLS and GCP are comparable:

storage_gb = number(total_3yr_storage) / 1073741824
storage_rate = ($0.023 * storage_gb) / month
storage_annual = storage_rate over 1 year

### How Databricks pricing works

Databricks charges in **DBUs (Databricks Units)** — an abstract
compute credit. A bigger cluster burns more DBUs per hour. The
price per DBU varies by workload type AND by contract tier.

**Important:** Each workload type has its own published DBU rate.
Here are the AWS Premium tier list prices (source:
[Mammoth Analytics pricing guide](https://mammoth.io/blog/databricks-pricing-2/)):

- **Jobs Compute:** $0.15/DBU — cheapest, for scheduled batch work
- **All-Purpose Compute:** $0.40/DBU — interactive notebooks
- **SQL Serverless:** $0.70/DBU — analyst SQL warehouses
- **SQL Classic:** $0.22/DBU — self-managed SQL warehouses

Premium is now the base tier on AWS and GCP (Standard was retired
Oct 2025). Enterprise adds ~15–25% to these rates but is not
publicly listed — it's a negotiated contract. We use Premium rates
as a conservative baseline. (Enterprise pricing is negotiated;
[CloudZero](https://www.cloudzero.com/blog/databricks-pricing/)
reports about $0.65/DBU for All-Purpose on Enterprise.)

### Compute: SQL Warehouse for 15 analysts

A SQL warehouse is a dedicated compute endpoint for SQL queries.
"Serverless" means Databricks manages the VMs — starts in seconds,
scales to zero when idle. You pay only while queries run.

Warehouse sizes are predefined (2X-Small through 4X-Large). A
"Medium" is appropriate for 15 concurrent analysts. It consumes
16 DBUs for each hour it's active. Serverless SQL is the most
expensive workload type at $0.70/DBU.

Not all 15 analysts query 8 hours/day. Realistic active compute
time — where the warehouse is actually processing, not idle
between queries — is ~6 hours of warehouse time per workday:

sql_dbu_rate = $0.70
sql_dbus_per_hour = 16
analyst_active_hours = 6
workdays_per_month = 22
sql_monthly = sql_dbu_rate * sql_dbus_per_hour * analyst_active_hours * workdays_per_month
sql_annual = sql_monthly * 12

### Compute: ETL pipeline jobs

ETL (Extract, Transform, Load) processes raw SCADA data into
cleaned, aggregated tables. It runs on a "Jobs" cluster —
non-interactive, optimized for scheduled work. Jobs Compute at
$0.15/DBU is the cheapest workload type.

Our wind utility runs a nightly batch pipeline (~1 hour) plus
hourly micro-batch streaming (~15 min each). Total effective
compute: ~4 hours/day. A small Jobs cluster (2–4 workers)
consumes about 10 DBUs/hour:

jobs_dbu_rate = $0.15
etl_dbus_per_hour = 10
etl_hours_per_day = 4
etl_daily = jobs_dbu_rate * etl_dbus_per_hour * etl_hours_per_day
etl_monthly = etl_daily * 30
etl_annual = etl_monthly * 12

### Compute: ML training & serving

**Training:** Retrain the vibration/bearing-failure model weekly
on historical data. Needs a GPU cluster (1–2 NVIDIA A10G or T4
instances), ~8 hours per session. Uses All-Purpose Compute at
$0.40/DBU because data scientists need interactive access during
model development. 24 DBUs/hour for a GPU-equipped cluster:

ml_train_rate = $0.40
ml_training_dbus_hour = 24
ml_training_hours_week = 8
ml_training_weekly = ml_train_rate * ml_training_dbus_hour * ml_training_hours_week
ml_training_monthly = ml_training_weekly * 4

**Serving:** A REST endpoint scoring incoming SCADA readings
against the trained model 24/7. Returns risk scores like "this
bearing has a 73% chance of failure in 48 hours." Minimal
compute — 4 DBUs/hour for a small Model Serving endpoint at
roughly All-Purpose rates:

ml_serve_rate = $0.40
ml_serving_dbus_hour = 4
ml_serving_daily = ml_serve_rate * ml_serving_dbus_hour * 24
ml_serving_monthly = ml_serving_daily * 30

ml_monthly = ml_training_monthly + ml_serving_monthly
ml_annual = ml_monthly * 12

### Cloud VMs & networking

Databricks DBU charges are NOT the whole bill. Databricks runs
on YOUR cloud account — clusters spin up EC2/Azure VMs, attach
disks, use networking. Those charges appear on your AWS/Azure/GCP
bill separately.

The ratio varies by workload (GPU instances cost more, serverless
SQL has lower VM overhead), but cloud infra typically adds 40–60%
on top of DBU spend (source:
[Dawiso](https://www.dawiso.com/glossary/databricks-pricing-explained-real-cost-breakdown-for-2025)
reports 50–200% of DBU charges). We use 50%:

dbu_annual = sql_annual + etl_annual + ml_annual
cloud_infra_annual = 50% of dbu_annual

### Databricks support

Enterprise support: 24/7 availability, 1-hour response SLA for
critical issues. Typically 10% of annual DBU spend (per standard
enterprise agreements — this is not publicly documented but is
widely reported in vendor comparison guides — Mammoth, CloudZero,
DoiT all cite 10%):

support_annual = 10% of dbu_annual

### Platform team

Two data engineers to maintain the platform: writing pipelines,
managing DLT jobs, responding to data quality alerts, handling
Unity Catalog permissions, fielding analyst requests. $160K/year
is a mid-range fully-loaded cost (salary + benefits + taxes) for
a US data engineer (BLS + Levels.fyi, $140K–$180K range):

platform_team_annual = $160K * 2

### Total Databricks costs

infra_annual = storage_annual + dbu_annual + cloud_infra_annual + support_annual
total_with_people = infra_annual + platform_team_annual

### Monthly run rates

infra_monthly = infra_annual / 12
total_monthly = total_with_people / 12

---

## Part 4: The DIY Alternative

What if you skip Databricks and build with open-source tools on
your own infrastructure?

### Compute server

A utility this size would run analytics on a dedicated server in
their data center or a cloud VM — not individual laptops. A
beefy analytics server (64-core, 512GB RAM, NVMe storage) costs
about $30K and lasts 4 years. Or a comparable cloud VM (e.g.,
r6i.16xlarge on AWS at about $3/hour on-demand) runs about
$3K/month:

onprem_server = $30000
onprem_annual = onprem_server / 4

Or as a cloud VM:

cloud_vm_monthly = $3000
cloud_vm_annual = cloud_vm_monthly * 12

We'll use the cloud VM since it's more comparable to Databricks
(both are cloud-hosted, similar availability):

diy_compute = cloud_vm_annual

### Platform tools

DuckDB is free, but it's a query engine, not a platform. To
match Databricks' capabilities you need separate tools:

- **Confluent Cloud** (about $2K/month): Managed Kafka for durable
  SCADA ingestion.
- **Data catalog/governance** (about $3K/month): Atlan, Alation, or
  similar for access control, lineage, audit trails. Required
  for NERC CIP compliance.
- **ML platform** (about $2K/month): Weights & Biases, SageMaker, or
  Vertex AI for experiment tracking and model
  serving.
- **Pipeline orchestration** (about $1K/month): Managed Airflow
  (Astronomer, MWAA) for scheduling and monitoring
  ETL.

These are managed SaaS tiers sized for a ~500-turbine operation.
Self-hosted would be cheaper but requires more ops headcount.

streaming_cost = $2000 * 12
governance_cost = $3000 * 12
ml_platform_cost = $2000 * 12
orchestration_cost = $1000 * 12
diy_tools = sum of streaming_cost, governance_cost, ml_platform_cost, orchestration_cost

### Integration engineer

Someone has to connect these 5 tools, maintain the glue code,
build the governance layer that doesn't exist natively across
them, and debug cross-system failures:

diy_engineer = $160K

### Total DIY

diy_total = diy_compute + diy_tools + diy_engineer
diy_monthly = diy_total / 12

---

## Part 5: Risk-Adjusted Business Case

The real question isn't "what does the platform cost?" It's
"what does NOT having the platform cost?"

### False alarm dispatches

Without a good ML model and data quality pipeline, anomaly
detection runs on simple thresholds (e.g., "alert if gearbox
temp > 80°C"). Simple thresholds produce many false positives.
Each false alarm dispatches a technician to a remote site: truck
roll, drive time, inspection, paperwork. Wind O&M site visits
average $37/kW (source: [NREL O&M study, 2023](https://docs.nrel.gov/docs/fy23osti/83712.pdf)); for a false alarm on a single turbine
component, $1,500–$2,500 is a reasonable estimate. 20/month is
conservative for a 500-turbine fleet running basic thresholds
(~1 false alarm per 25 turbines per month):

dispatch_cost = $2000
dispatches_per_month = 20
annual_false_alarms = dispatch_cost * dispatches_per_month * 12

### Missed bearing failures

A catastrophic bearing failure destroys the gearbox and can
damage the generator. Replacement cost: $200K–$800K including
the gearbox itself, crane rental at [$80K+/day](https://www.pruftechnik.com/wind-turbine-gearbox-alignment/),
labor, and lost generation revenue. [GM Insights](https://www.gminsights.com/industry-analysis/wind-turbine-gearbox-repair-and-refurbishment-market) reports $300K–$800K total. We use $500K.

Without predictive maintenance, 2 missed failures/year across
500 turbines is conservative — that's a 0.4% annual failure rate.
EPRI reports fleet gearbox failure rates of 0.9% in years 1–4,
rising with age, and 30–70% cumulative over 20 years
([EPRI data](https://wifitalents.com/wind-turbine-failure-statistics/)).

turbine_cost = $500K
missed_per_year = 2
annual_missed_failures = turbine_cost * missed_per_year

### NERC CIP violation

NERC enforces CIP (Critical Infrastructure Protection) standards.
The maximum penalty is $1.54M per violation per day as of
2025 ([NERC Sanction Guidelines](https://www.nerc.com/pa/Stand/Resources/Documents/Appendix_4B_of_the_Rules_of_Procedure_Sanction_Guidelines.pdf)). Duke Energy was [fined $10M](https://www.utilitydive.com/news/duke-fined-10m-for-cybersecurity-lapses-since-2015/547528/) in 2019 for 127 cybersecurity violations over 3 years. A single
audit finding for inadequate access controls on CEII typically
results in a $500K–$1M settlement. We use $1M as a "one bad
audit" scenario:

nerc_fine = $1M

total_risk = annual_false_alarms + annual_missed_failures + nerc_fine

### Platform availability

Data gaps mean blind spots for anomaly detection:

good_uptime = downtime(99.9%, 1 year)
great_uptime = downtime(99.99%, 1 year)

At 99.9% you lose good_uptime of data/year — enough to miss
a bearing failure developing over a weekend. At 99.99% it's
great_uptime.

